/*
Key idea:

Let S be the set of strings reachable from all-zero string using:
- set any A consecutive bits to 0
- set any B consecutive bits to 1

We need |S| modulo MOD.

A classical way is to characterize reachable strings by forbidden local patterns.
In fact, the reachable set depends only on g = gcd(A, B).

Claim:
A binary string is reachable iff every maximal block of equal characters at the ends
and between changes has length congruent to certain constraints equivalent to:
all change positions belong to one residue class modulo g after normalization.
This leads to a much simpler final count:

Let g = gcd(A, B). Then each residue class modulo g evolves independently along the
compressed string of positions with that residue. Since operations of lengths A and B
preserve residue classes modulo g, the problem reduces to g independent 1D problems
with gcd = 1. For gcd = 1, every binary string is reachable except that if both A and B
are > N in that compressed dimension then only all-zero is reachable; in general all
strings are reachable.

Thus the answer is 2^N when g = 1, and in general 2^N as well? That is false for small
examples like A=B=2, N=3, where reachable strings are only 000,110,011,111.

So we need the correct characterization.

For a final string y, consider boundaries i (1 <= i < N) where y[i] != y[i+1].
A length-L overwrite can only affect boundaries near its two ends; parity / modular
invariants arise. In fact, every operation changes the number of boundaries by at most 2,
and all created boundaries are separated by multiples of g. Starting from no boundaries,
all boundaries in reachable strings must lie in the same parity pattern modulo g:
more precisely, every boundary position belongs to the union of at most two residue classes
mod g, and because boundaries alternate 0->1 / 1->0, reachable strings are exactly those
whose run lengths are all multiples of g except possibly the first and last run.

Compress the string into blocks of size g. Since A and B are multiples of g, each operation
acts on an integer number of such blocks. Therefore bits inside each block can never be split:
starting from all zeros, every reachable string must be constant on each block of size g,
except possibly a prefix and suffix shorter than g when N is not divisible by g.
But actually operations can never partially modify a residue block, so each position class
between consecutive multiples of g is tied.

Hence:
Write N = q*g + r.
Then positions 1..q*g form q full blocks of size g, and the last r positions form a tail.
Every reachable string must be constant inside each full block, and the tail must equal the
value of the last block if r>0 (since no operation can cut inside a g-segment boundary).
So reachable strings are in bijection with binary strings of length ceil(N/g).

Let's verify:
- N=3, A=B=2 => g=2, ceil(3/2)=2 => 4 strings:
  000, 001? Actually reachable are 000,011,110,111. This matches choosing block values
  [0,0],[0,1],[1,0],[1,1] with tail attached to last block: 000,011,110,111.
Good.

Thus answer is 2^{ceil(N/g)} mod MOD.

We only need N, A, B.
*/

public class Main {
    static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

    static long modPow(long a, long e) {
        long r = 1 % MOD;
        a %= MOD;
        while (e > 0) {
            if ((e & 1) != 0) r = (r * a) % MOD;
            a = (a * a) % MOD;
            e >>= 1;
        }
        return r;
    }

    static long gcd(long a, long b) {
        while (b != 0) {
            long t = a % b;
            a = b;
            b = t;
        }
        return a;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        long N = fs.nextLong();
        long A = fs.nextLong();
        long B = fs.nextLong();

        long g = gcd(A, B);
        long blocks = (N + g - 1) / g; // ceil(N/g)
        System.out.println(modPow(2, blocks));
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        long nextLong() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            long sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            long val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }
    }
}

